      "title": "Where you are on the ship",
      "minutes": 15,
      "tagline": "SS Vibelandia · Reading Room · Canvas · QUESTFEST",
      "goals": [
        "Point to the Reading Room for papers and this course for guided study.",
        "Name the site’s front door (Omniversal Canvas) and the main decks.",
        "Ask the three orientation questions of any new deck you visit."
      ],
      "body": "<p>Every good hotel puts a map by the elevator with a small red dot: <em>you are here</em>. Without the dot, the map is trivia; with it, the map is power. This lesson is your red dot. SS Vibelandia is framed as a holographic resort vessel — hospitality, marketplace, nightlife, and brotherhood as voyage identity — and its grand arc runs Genesis → Borikén → Reno (432 Hz · QUESTFEST 24×365). You do not need the whole legend today. You need to know which deck you are standing on and where the doors lead.</p>\n<h3>The decks you will actually use</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Omniversal Canvas</strong> (<code>/</code>) — the site’s front door, where Valet Pru welcomes arrivals. Everything else is reachable from here.</li>\n<li><strong>Reading Room</strong> — the shelves themselves: concert programs and the full whitepaper catalog. This is where primary sources live.</li>\n<li><strong>This course</strong> — the structured path through those papers: the guide from Lesson 1.1, walking you room by room.</li>\n<li><strong>Lattice Chat</strong> — BYOK conversation on the Infinite Octaves nest, where you can ask questions of the corpus directly (Week 2 explains BYOK).</li>\n<li><strong>QUESTFEST board</strong> — the ship’s bulletin board and latest ship notes, where new papers are announced in plain language.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notice the division of labour. The Reading Room is a library; this course is a syllabus; Lattice Chat is office hours; the QUESTFEST board is the departmental newsletter. Each door answers a different kind of curiosity, and knowing which door matches which question saves you real time. One habit worth adopting from day one: when a new paper ships, it appears on the QUESTFEST board with a plain-language note before anything else — newest first — so a weekly glance at the board keeps you current without wading through the full shelves.</p>\n<h3>Three questions for any deck</h3>\n<p>When you step onto an unfamiliar surface anywhere on the ship, ask: <em>Where am I? What can I do here? How do I stay Goldilocks?</em> The first question is answered by the page’s own header and honesty band. The second, by its visible actions — read, listen, ask, browse. The third is the habit from Lesson 1.3: remember which claims are story, which are catalog, and which are software, and remember who outranks all of them (people do). Those three questions turn any confusing page into a navigable one in under a minute.</p>\n<div class=\"worked-example\">\n<p>Practise a real route. Suppose Week 3 leaves you wanting the full prime-parity filing, not the lesson summary. The route: from any lesson, the <strong>Further reading</strong> panel links the paper directly — one click. Alternatively: Reading Room → whitepapers shelf → find “Prime-parity framework” → open, then read the honesty boundary first (Lesson 1.3 habit). Now the reverse route: you are deep in a paper and want the guided context again. The course link in the ship’s navigation returns you here, and your lesson position is exactly where you left it, saved on this device. Two routes, both under thirty seconds. Orientation is a skill you now have.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"misconception\">\n<p><strong>The mix-up:</strong> “The ship, the voyage, the valet — this is all decoration around the real content, so I can ignore it.”</p>\n<p><strong>The correction:</strong> The voyage frame is a labelled narrative tier doing structural work: it carries belonging, hospitality, and the Goldilocks ethic that governs how the technical material may be used. Ignoring it is like ignoring a museum’s wayfinding because you only came for one painting — you will get lost, and you will miss the reason the collection is arranged as it is.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"key-terms\"><dl>\n<dt>Omniversal Canvas</dt><dd>The site’s front door at the root path — the welcome surface from which every other deck is reachable.</dd>\n<dt>Reading Room</dt><dd>The primary-source deck: whitepaper shelves and concert programs, unmediated by lesson summaries.</dd>\n<dt>QUESTFEST board</dt><dd>The ship’s bulletin board — plain-language ship notes announcing new papers, newest first.</dd>\n</dl></div>",
