      "body": "<p>Listen to a song you love and notice what the chorus does. It returns — but never quite identically: the third chorus carries the weight of the verses between, a new harmony, a lifted key. A chorus is not a photocopy; it is a <strong>rhyme at the scale of structure</strong> — recognisable identity, renewed each time, pulling the whole song back toward its centre. The Omni-Lattice teaches fractals exactly this way. Forget the frozen poster of the Mandelbrot set for a moment: under Φ, a fractal is filed as a <strong>holographic rhyme</strong> — a <em>motion</em> grammar, something patterns do over time and depth, not just a shape they hold in a frame.</p>\n<h3>The four pillars</h3>\n<p>The rhyme paper locks the definition to four pillars, and the course will reuse all four constantly, so take them slowly:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Repeating</strong> — the pattern recurs across scales and occasions, like the chorus returning. Without repetition there is nothing to recognise.</li>\n<li><strong>Self-similar</strong> — parts echo the whole: a branch resembles its tree, a cove resembles its coastline, one lesson of this course resembles the course’s whole rhythm. The echo is structural, not photocopied.</li>\n<li><strong>Self-correcting</strong> — drift gets pulled back toward form. A jazz band wanders and returns to the head; a healthy process notices its deviation and compensates. This is the pillar a static picture cannot show.</li>\n<li><strong>Recursive</strong> — the same operator nests inside itself: the rule that builds the branch also builds the twig, powered in this catalog by the Φ key from Lesson 3.1.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Why “holographic”? In a hologram, each fragment of the plate encodes a view of the entire scene — the whole lives in every part. That is the Seed:Edge intuition running through the corpus: any well-made piece (a lesson, a paper, a protocol) should carry the pattern of the whole architecture within it. The rhyme grammar is how the catalog names that property and checks for it.</p>\n<h3>More dimensions, same rails</h3>\n<p>A companion paper extends the rhyme across dimensional talk with <strong>cross-scale summation</strong> written xD ± yD — patterns at one dimensional register composing with patterns at another, added or subtracted like interfering waves. File this exactly as the honesty rail files it: <em>catalog encoding</em> — a bookkeeping grammar for how filings at different scales combine — and not a statement about spacetime, and certainly not a falsification or confirmation of holographic principles in physics (AdS/CFT keeps its own literature, untouched).</p>\n<div class=\"worked-example\">\n<p>Audit a fern frond against all four pillars — pencil optional, houseplant helpful. <strong>Repeating:</strong> the frond’s leaflet pattern recurs up and down the stem — check. <strong>Self-similar:</strong> each leaflet is a small frond, its own sub-leaflets arranged like the parent’s — check. <strong>Self-correcting:</strong> grow the plant toward a window and rotate the pot: new growth redirects toward the light, drift pulled back toward viable form — check. <strong>Recursive:</strong> one growth rule, applied at frond, leaflet, and sub-leaflet scale alike — check. Four for four: the fern earns the rhyme label. Counter-audit: a brick wall. Repeating, yes. Self-similar? No — a brick does not resemble the wall. Self-correcting? No — knock out a brick and nothing compensates. The wall fails at pillar two and stays merely periodic. The audit is the skill: rhyme is a four-part test, not a vibe.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"misconception\">\n<p><strong>The mix-up:</strong> “Fractal talk plus the word ‘holographic’ means the corpus is doing AdS/CFT physics — or retiring it.”</p>\n<p><strong>The correction:</strong> The rhyme grammar is a filing definition with four checkable pillars, used to organise and audit patterns in the catalog. The physics holographic principle is a separate body of work that this corpus neither performs nor challenges — the honesty rail on the rhyme papers says so directly.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"key-terms\"><dl>\n<dt>Holographic rhyme</dt><dd>The catalog’s four-pillar definition of fractal behaviour as motion grammar: repeating, self-similar, self-correcting, recursive.</dd>\n<dt>Self-correcting</dt><dd>The dynamic pillar: deviation from form gets pulled back — the property that distinguishes living rhyme from frozen pattern.</dd>\n<dt>Cross-scale summation (xD ± yD)</dt><dd>Bookkeeping grammar for combining filings at different dimensional registers — catalog encoding, not spacetime physics.</dd>\n</dl></div>",
