      "body": "<p>A carpenter finishing an apprenticeship owns exactly three tools that matter more than the rest of the box: something to measure with, something to join with, something to check squareness with. Week 3 has been your apprenticeship week, and you now own the catalog’s equivalent trio. Before the course moves into rooms furnished with heavier vocabulary — voids, crystals, Higgs gates — this short lesson lays the three tools side by side, shows them working as one motion, and tells you where each will be needed again.</p>\n<h3>The toolkit, restated</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Φ, the nesting key</strong> (Lesson 3.1) — the gear ratio of the cabinet. Φ² = Φ + 1 makes the shelves self-consistent at every depth. Honest use: architectural scaling. Forbidden use: substituting for ℏ, c, or G.</li>\n<li><strong>Primes, the addresses</strong> (Lesson 3.2) — sole-even 2 anchoring all pairness; odd primes as unforgeable vaults. Honest use: irreducible labelling. Forbidden use: attributing physical powers to prime-numbered things.</li>\n<li><strong>Rhyme, the audit</strong> (Lesson 3.3) — the four-pillar test (repeating, self-similar, self-correcting, recursive) for whether a pattern genuinely carries the whole in its parts. Honest use: structural auditing. Forbidden use: claiming or denying holographic physics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notice how the three interlock: Φ says <em>how</em> shelves scale, primes say <em>which address</em> a filing gets, and rhyme says <em>whether</em> the nested structure is healthy — measure, join, check squareness. Every big construction later in the course — the singularity crystal in Week 4, the Fibonacci vault ladder in Week 6 — is these three tools working together, and if a later lesson ever feels dense, the reliable remedy is to return here and restate the trio in your own words.</p>\n<h3>A retrieval exercise before you move on</h3>\n<p>Learning science has one boring, overwhelming finding: retrieval beats rereading. So before Week 4, close your eyes (or look away from the screen) and answer three questions from memory. What identity defines Φ, and why does it matter for shelves? Why is 2 the only even prime, and what job does the catalog give that fact? Which rhyme pillar can a photograph never show? If any answer would not survive a sceptical friend, revisit that one lesson now — five minutes today saves thirty in Week 4, where all three tools go straight to work on voids and crystals.</p>\n<div class=\"worked-example\">\n<p>Run the full toolkit on one fresh sentence from Week 6’s territory: “Chordata files at vaults V₃₄ and V₅₅ on the Fibonacci ladder.” <strong>Tool one, Φ:</strong> the ladder’s rungs grow by Fibonacci numbers, whose consecutive ratios (34/21 ≈ 1.619, 55/34 ≈ 1.617) approach Φ — so the ladder is a Φ-nested structure; the key applies. <strong>Tool two, primes and irreducibility:</strong> the vault labels are Fibonacci indices used as address sets — irreducible filing labels in the vault grammar, chosen for their nesting arithmetic. <strong>Tool three, rhyme:</strong> does the ladder rhyme? Rungs repeat, each rung’s structure echoes the ladder, the labelling rule is one recursion applied at every level — pillars check. Verdict: a well-formed <em>catalog</em> construction. And the tier stamp, by reflex now: nothing in that audit sequenced any DNA. Addresses, ratios, and audits — all filing.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"misconception\">\n<p><strong>The mix-up:</strong> “These three tools are separate topics I can learn independently and mix freely with physics claims.”</p>\n<p><strong>The correction:</strong> They are one toolkit with one shared honesty rail. Φ scales the shelves that primes address and rhyme audits — and all three live on the catalog tier together. Lifting any one of them across the physics line breaks the same boundary, no matter which tool you lifted.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"key-terms\"><dl>\n<dt>Week 3 toolkit</dt><dd>The trio Φ (nesting key) · primes (addresses) · rhyme (audit) — measure, join, and check for every catalog construction in the rest of the course.</dd>\n<dt>Fibonacci–Φ link</dt><dd>Consecutive Fibonacci ratios (34/21, 55/34, …) converge to Φ — the arithmetic bridge between the vault ladder of Week 6 and the nesting key of Week 3.</dd>\n</dl></div>",
