      "body": "<p>Drag a spoon through water and it glides; drag it through honey and the world pushes back. Viscosity is the fluid’s resistance to flow — the property that turns a fast gesture into a slow, deliberate one. Now borrow that feeling for something stranger. Ideas, intentions, plans: in imagination they move instantly, everywhere at once, unlocated. But the moment an intention becomes an <em>act</em> — a word spoken, a keystroke, a thing built — it slows to the pace of the physical world, and nothing physical outruns the speed of light. The Viscosity of Light shelf files that transition with one vivid image: <strong>c as a frictional floor</strong> — the honey through which the instant, unlocated stuff of thought must move to become local, embodied story.</p>\n<h3>What the image organises</h3>\n<p>Week 5 is the corpus’s physical-vocabulary wing, and this lesson sets the reading posture for all four rooms in it: every shelf here is a <strong>metaphor with rails</strong> — a physical image doing organisational work, wearing an honesty rail that keeps it from moonlighting as physics. The viscosity image earns its shelf because it makes one real pattern discussable: the asymmetry between how freely we ideate and how slowly we embody. Planning a novel takes an afternoon; writing it takes years. In catalog terms: <strong>non-local intent meets viscosity at c and becomes local story</strong>. The image gives conversations about attention, creativity, and embodiment a shared handle — that is a filing job, and it is done well.</p>\n<h3>Where the edge lies</h3>\n<p>Now the rails, stated with the same care. “Cognitive non-locality” in this shelf’s vocabulary is <strong>Soft Story</strong> (Week 4’s tier): a way of speaking about how thought <em>feels</em> unlocated — not a claim that thought physically outruns light, and not a loophole for faster-than-light messaging. Relativity is not under review here. Light’s actual speed in vacuum is a constant, not a fluid drag coefficient; real photons in real media slow for reasons optics textbooks explain without any help from this catalog. The metaphor’s edge is exactly where a reader might try to send a signal with it — and at that edge, the honesty rail stands.</p>\n<div class=\"worked-example\">\n<p>File a wild claim through the shelf, step by step. The claim: <em>“Since thought is non-local, meditation lets you communicate faster than light.”</em> <strong>Step 1 — find the borrowed image:</strong> the claim leans on this shelf’s “non-local intent” vocabulary. <strong>Step 2 — check the tier label:</strong> the shelf files cognitive non-locality as Soft Story — a phenomenology of how ideation feels, explicitly not a physical transport mechanism. <strong>Step 3 — test against the rail:</strong> the claim asserts signalling, i.e. physics. The rail says: no FTL thought, no relativity retirement. <strong>Step 4 — verdict:</strong> the claim commits a tier upgrade — it lifted a filing metaphor across the physics line. Correct filing: “the felt instantaneity of thought is real as experience and filed as story; any signalling claim needs physics evidence this corpus neither has nor pretends to have.” Notice the method never required mocking the claimant — just the label check. That courtesy-with-rigour is the Week 5 posture.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"misconception\">\n<p><strong>The mix-up:</strong> “A paper called Viscosity of Light must be proposing new optics — a measurable drag on photons.”</p>\n<p><strong>The correction:</strong> The title names a metaphor: c as the frictional floor where intent becomes embodied act. No optical measurement is proposed or claimed; real photon physics stays in the optics literature. File the shelf as catalog imagery for localisation, wearing its rail.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"key-terms\"><dl>\n<dt>Frictional floor (c)</dt><dd>The metaphor filing the speed of light as the pace limit where non-local ideation becomes local, embodied story.</dd>\n<dt>Cognitive non-locality</dt><dd>Soft-Story vocabulary for the felt instantaneity and unlocatedness of thought — phenomenology, never a signalling mechanism.</dd>\n<dt>Metaphor with rails</dt><dd>Week 5’s standard object: a physical image doing organisational work, bounded by an explicit honesty rail against physics upgrade.</dd>\n</dl></div>",
