      "id": "companions",
      "moduleId": "week6",
      "week": 6,
      "part": "Homework: Week 6",
      "partId": "week6",
      "number": "6.4",
      "title": "Application companions · Not engine pins",
      "minutes": 16,
      "tagline": "PDVSA gateway · macro-protein · FractiSkills · SNA↔TCP/IP rhyme",
      "goals": [
        "Keep application companions off the engine pin list, and say why the list matters.",
        "File the PDVSA gateway demo and the SNA↔TCP/IP rhyme at their stated tiers.",
        "Read the macro-protein θ_bio ∈ [13,17] band as catalog framing over published scaling laws.",
        "Place FractiSkills as the portable SKILL.md lab pack under the AI catalog layer — consumed by Lattice Chat, never an engine pin."
      ],
      "body": "<p>A well-run hardware store keeps two kinds of display. The <strong>wall of tools</strong> — the permanent, load-bearing inventory every project relies on — and the <strong>demo table</strong> by the entrance, where this month a jig shows what the tools can build. Nobody confuses them: the demo table changes; the wall is the store. The Omni-Lattice corpus draws exactly this line between <strong>engine pins</strong> (the shelf of papers this course has toured — the map, Φ, primes, the crystal, the CMOS bridge) and <strong>application companions</strong>: demonstration papers showing the engine grammar at work in specific domains. Companions are honest, often vivid work — and they are never pins. The sync documents mark them “application companion (not engine pin)” in so many words, because a reader who mistakes a demo for load-bearing inventory will misjudge the whole store.</p>\n<h3>The gateway demo</h3>\n<p>The <strong>PDVSA Gateway Ops</strong> companion is a multi-domain executive demonstration: an incident-operations brief for an oil-and-gas enterprise setting, showing how Lattice-style routing grammar organises a complex operational picture. Its historical spine is a genuinely good rhyme: <strong>IBM SNA ↔ TCP/IP</strong> — the era when enterprises ran centralised, hierarchical networking (SNA) and had to bridge into the open, packet-switched internet world. Legacy-hierarchy-meets-open-lattice is the rhyme; the gateway is the moral. And the tier is stamped on the tin: <em>mockup</em>. No live oilfield telemetry flows anywhere in it; wells, pressures, and incidents in the brief are simulator and catalog map, full stop.</p>\n<h3>The work-engine demo</h3>\n<p>The <strong>macro-protein work engine</strong> companion demonstrates the grammar on living systems: organisms filed as work engines, with a characteristic band <strong>θ<sub>bio</sub> ∈ [13, 17]</strong> as the catalog’s organismal register. Its external locks are the real, published scaling laws of biology — <strong>Kleiber’s law</strong> (metabolic rate scaling roughly as mass to the ¾ power) and the <strong>WBE model</strong> that grounds it in branching transport networks. Read the lock direction the way Lesson 6.3 taught: the published laws anchor the imagery; the companion re-derives nothing and adds no biology. The θ band is filing vocabulary — an address range, not a measured constant of life.</p>\n<h3>The portable-skills lab pack</h3>\n<p>The <strong>FractiSkills</strong> companion is the course’s runnable homework drawer under the <strong>AI catalog layer</strong>: one portable <code>SKILL.md</code> brief per SS Vibelandia surface, generated locally from registries into <code>skills/</code>, then loaded pointer-first into Lattice Chat (≤8 pointers, ≤2 pinches — never a fat-dump of the library). Credit the original portable-skills grammar to Daniel Ari Friedman’s FractiSkills paper and repo; the SING 13 filing is the local Lattice Chat companion that consumes that idea. Honesty rails stay loud: not Skillarum embed, not live public-crawl proof, not an engine pin, not a replacement for whitepapers. Students who want a lab after Lesson 6.2’s catalog-layer story open FractiSkills, regenerate the index, and watch nested agents inherit pointers instead of pasting the site.</p>\n<div class=\"worked-example\">\n<p>Sort a mixed stack of seven papers into wall versus demo table — the librarian’s minute. (1) CMOS/protonic bridge: pinned at catalogPriority 0 — <strong>wall</strong> (the pin, in fact). (2) PDVSA Gateway Ops: marked application companion, simulator-tier — <strong>demo table</strong>. (3) Prime-parity framework: core Week 3 grammar every later paper reuses — <strong>wall</strong>. (4) Macro-protein work engine: domain demonstration over published scaling locks — <strong>demo table</strong>. (5) Holographic Singularity Crystal: engine construction with node k = 0 fixtures — <strong>wall</strong>. (6) SNA↔TCP/IP rhyme: historical spine for the gateway demo — <strong>demo table</strong> (it serves the companion). (7) FractiSkills: portable SKILL.md corpus under the AI catalog layer — <strong>demo table</strong> (lab pack companions consume the catalog; they do not redefine the engine shelf). Now the payoff question: <em>why does the sort matter?</em> Because evaluation order and trust flow from it — auditors judge the engine by its pins; demos borrow credibility from the wall and must never lend it backward. Mixing the lists is how catalogs lose the trust the honesty rails earned.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"misconception\">\n<p><strong>The mix-up:</strong> “The PDVSA brief means the corpus operates live oilfield infrastructure — real telemetry, real incidents, a production deployment. FractiSkills means Skillarum is embedded and the public site was crawled into finished physics.”</p>\n<p><strong>The correction:</strong> PDVSA is a mockup by its own label: simulator data and catalog mapping arranged as an executive demonstration. FractiSkills is a local, regenerable skill corpus with pointer-first Lattice Chat load — related-work credit to Friedman’s paper/repo, honesty rails refusing Skillarum embed and live-crawl proof. Demo table, clearly signed.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"key-terms\"><dl>\n<dt>Engine pin</dt><dd>A load-bearing paper on the engine shelf — the permanent inventory by which auditors judge the corpus, ordered by catalog priority.</dd>\n<dt>Application companion</dt><dd>A demonstration paper showing engine grammar in a specific domain — vivid, honest, and explicitly never a pin.</dd>\n<dt>FractiSkills</dt><dd>Portable <code>SKILL.md</code> packages under the AI catalog layer — pointer-first Lattice Chat load; lab pack, not engine identity.</dd>\n<dt>SNA ↔ TCP/IP rhyme</dt><dd>The historical spine of the gateway demo: hierarchical enterprise networking bridging into the open internet — legacy-meets-lattice as teaching rhyme.</dd>\n<dt>θ_bio ∈ [13,17]</dt><dd>The organismal address band of the macro-protein companion — filing vocabulary over published scaling laws (Kleiber/WBE), not a measured constant.</dd>\n</dl></div>",
